speechtext:
The Budget-Mr. Caldwell
The Crowsnest pass agreement was an undertaking entered into in 1897 between the government of Canada and the Canadian Pacific Railway, whereby in consideration of a subsidy to be earned by the construction of the Crowsnest pass railway, which subsidy amounted to $3,404,720, a reduction should be made in the rates upon the classes of merchandise.
The Crowsnest pass agreement was an undertaking entered into in 1887 between the government of Canada and the Canadian Pacific Railway Company, whereby in consideration of a subsidy to be earned by the construction of the Crowsnest pass railway, which subsidy amounted to $3,404,720. a reduction should be made in the rates upon the classes of merchandise below stated westbound from, and including Fort William and all points east of Fort William on the companies railway-
I want the House to pay particular attention to this.
-to all points west of Fort William on the company's main line or on any line of railway throughout Canada, owned or leased by, or operated on account of the company. The commodities, the classes of merchandise to be affected, are as follows:-
Upon all green and fresh fruits, 33£ per cent;
Coal oil, 20 per cent;
Cordage and binder twine, 10 per cent;
Agricultural implements of all kinds, set up in parts, 10 per cent;
Iron, including bar, band, Canada pilates, galvanized,	sheets, pipe,	pipe-fittings,	nails, spikes	and
horse shoes, 10 per cent;
All kinds of wire, 10 per cent;
Window glass, 10 per cent;
Paper	for	building	and roofing	purposes, 10	per
cent;
Roofing felt, box and packing, 10 per cent;
Paints of all kinds and oils, 10 per cent;
Live stock, 10 per cent;
Woodenware, 10 per cent;
Household furniture, 10 per cent;
The agreement also provided for a rduction in the rates on grain and flour from all points on the main line, branches or connections of the Canadian Pacific Railway	west	of Fort	William and	Port Arthur.	Thedecrease	was	fixed at	3 cents per hundred pounds to
be reached in two annual reductions of a cent and a half each.
Another contention has been made that the Crowsnest pass agreement provided for a rate on grain away below the cost of hauling. I think I can prove conclusively that that is not the case, and I wish to quote briefly from the report of the Transportation committee of this House of 1922:
The Crowsnest rate on grain and grain products remained in entirety from September 1, 1899, until October 7. 1903, when it was modified by a lower rate resulting from an agreement made between the province of Manitoba and the Canadian Northern Railway. Lower rates continued until March 15, 1918, when an increase of 15 per cent on rates generally was made by order of the Board of Railway Commissioners. This increase was granted because of representations by the railways that they could not successfully maintain their services unless granted rates that would enable them to meet the increased operating costs due to substantial increases in cost of fuel, coal materials, supplies, equipment of all kinds and wages.
Now comes the interesting part of it.
This increase was subject to the Crowsnest pass agreement, but it brought western grain rates up to
139i
Crowsnest maximum rates from most points in Saskatchewan and Alberta, though not in Manitoba.
That proves that for fifteen years the Canadian Pacific Railway hauled grain at least fifteen per cent and in some cases more than fifteen per cent below the rates provided in the Crowsnest pass agreement. I think this effectively explodes the theory that the Crowsnest rate was below what the railways could afford to haul it for, because there was nothing to compel the Canadian Pacific Railway to haul this grain at a lower rate than the Crowsnest rate. Yet we have the evidence here in the report of the committee that dealt with this matter two years ago that for fifteen years, from 1903 to 1918, the Canadian Pacific hauled this grain at rates at least fifteen per cent lower than the rate set by the Crowsnest pass agreement.
Now I wish to show exactly what these reductions mean to the eastern manufacturer and apple grower because to my mind that was a wise agreement. It was entered into tolenable the eastern manufacturer to get into the western market with his farm machinery, binder twine, wire fencing, horse shoes, spikes, nails and other commodities; and by the way, our friends to my right, the official opposition, while they are the champions of these interests, did not seem very favourable to the restoration of the Crowsnest pass agreement. They were even willing to sacrifice their manufacturing friends if they could deal a knockout to the western grain growers. That seemed to me to be the attitude when this matter was discussed in the House two years ago.
On page 21 of this report you will find a statement by the 'Board of Railway Commissioners, called statement No. 2, showing rates on agricultural implements, in carloads, from points in eastern Canada to various stations in western Canada. From the Toronto group, prior to the Crowsnest pass agreement, there was a rate of 76 cents per 100 pounds. Under the Crowsnest pass agreement it was reduced to 68i cents per 100 pounds, or a reduction of 7i cents per 100 pounds while the reduction on wheat was only 3 cents per hundred pounds.
From the Montreal group, prior to the Crowsnest pass agreement, the rate was 82 cents. That was reduced under the Crowsnest pass agreement to 74 cents, or 8 cents under the rate prior to that. On September 1, 1919, the rate in the Montreal group was further reduced to 62 cents per 100 pounds, although the railway was not compelled to give that rate under the Crowsnest pass agreement. That meant a reduction of 20 cents per 100 pounds. There is quite a long table here
The Budget-Mr. Caldwell
